{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These also are for the wise — to show partiality in judgment is not good</b> (Proverbs 24:23)." "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, arranged his proverbs and admonished with his words at the beginning of the book up to this point for the general category of the simple and the young, and so he declared at the beginning of the book that his proverbs are ״to give prudence to the simple, to the young man knowledge and discretion.״ And now he admonishes the wise, those who sit in judgment — this is what he said (Proverbs 24:23): ״Also these,״ that is, these proverbs too are for the wise, to admonish them with them. And what is the admonition? ״To show partiality in judgment is not good״ — he means, this is not a good trait among the wise, that they should show partiality in judgment. And even though the Torah warned concerning this (Deuteronomy 1:17): ״לא תכירו פנים במשפט,״ Solomon innovated here, for the Torah warned but did not specify punishment, and Solomon came and taught the punishment, saying that besides the fact that he transgresses the commandment of the Holy One, blessed be He, and is abhorrent to YHWH, he is further abhorrent to the people, for all will curse him. Therefore he immediately appended to it: ״To the wicked you say, 'You are righteous' — peoples will curse him, nations will abhor him״ — for a judge who says to the wicked in his case, 'You are righteous,' acquitting him when he is liable, how much more so if he convicts the one who is innocent — the multitude of the people will abhor him and distance him for perverting justice. And since the entire Torah from Genesis until לעיני כל ישראל depends on justice, therefore he said בל טוב and did not say לא טוב, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 147:20): ״ומשפטים בל ידעום.״" "<b>And know</b> that justice is the foundation of the Throne of Glory, as it is said (Psalms 89:14): ״צדק ומשפט מכון כסאך״ — and whoever upholds justice upholds the Throne, and whoever perverts justice and mars it mars the Throne. Therefore, for one who shows partiality in judgment, בל טוב — he does not merit the hidden good written of it (Psalms 31:19): ״מה רב טובך אשר צפנת וגו׳.״ And Solomon taught us here that one who shows partiality in judgment is punished in this world and in the world to come. Justice is the cause of peace, and therefore we find with Jethro that the counsel he gave Moses concerning judgment included the mention of peace, as it is written (Exodus 18:23): ״אם את הדבר הזה תעשה וצוך אלהים וגו׳ על מקומו יבא בשלום.״ And peace is the maintenance of the world, and therefore justice was entrusted to the wise, for the wise increase peace in the world, and consequently it is forbidden to bring a case except before the wise of Israel. And this is what is written." "<b>And these are the judgments which you shall set before them.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Gittin, chapter המגרש: ״לפניהם״ — and not before laymen. And Rashi of blessed memory explained there in his commentary: לפניהם — before the seventy of the Sanhedrin who stood at the mountain before the giving of the Torah, as it is written (Exodus 24:1): ״ואל משה אמר עלה אל ה' אתה ואהרן וגו׳.״ And they conclude there: even though we are laymen, we act as their agents. And since the verse should properly have said ״אשר תשים להם,״ in the manner of what is written (Exodus 15:25): ״שם שם לו חק ומשפט,״ therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory needed to expound: לפניהם — and not before gentiles — to teach that it is forbidden to go and litigate in gentile courts, even if their laws are like our laws." "<b>And know</b> that anyone who forsakes the laws of Israel and litigates in gentile courts — this is a single transgression that is the root of many transgressions, for from its root sprout and multiply branches of great sins, and in its wings are found harms and stumbling-blocks without end. And I shall explain to you how. Know that murder is among the most severe transgressions in the Torah, and its punishment is very great and severe, because he spills human blood and destroys one soul from the world — and he has not destroyed the murdered person alone, but also those who would have issued from him, and they have no end, for behold, the first man was singular, and all the creatures of the seventy languages that exist in the world are all his descendants; if so, you have it: whoever destroys one soul is as if he destroyed an entire world, and likewise regarding rescue and beneficence — whoever sustains one soul is as if he sustained an entire world. From this you learn how great is the sin of murder. Even though the sin of murder is severe, the sin of robbery and the profanation of HaShem's name is more severe than it, for the murderer is forgiven through repentance, as we find with Cain — but not so robbery: repentance does not avail him until he restores the stolen property, and if he does not restore it, he is never forgiven. And likewise the profanation of HaShem's name: repentance does not avail in it, for it is more severe than כריתות and court-imposed deaths, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: one who transgresses כריתות and court-imposed deaths and repents — repentance and Yom ha-Kippurim suspend, and suffering cleanses, as it is said (Psalms 89): ״ופקדתי בשבט פשעם ובנגעים עונם״; but one who has the profanation of HaShem's name in his hand — there is no power in repentance to suspend, nor in Yom ha-Kippurim to atone, nor in suffering to cleanse; rather, all of them suspend, and death cleanses, as it is said (Isaiah 22:14): ״ונגלה באזני ה' צבאות אם יכופר וגו׳.״ And these two transgressions that are more severe than murder — namely, robbery and the profanation of HaShem's name — are both included in the sin of gentile courts. Profanation of HaShem's name: for this person most certainly profanes the name and gives honor to another, and his praise to idols, and it is a profanation of HaShem's name by treating idolatry as of account. And Scripture says (Deuteronomy 32:31): ״כי לא כצורנו צורם ואויבינו פלילים״ — that is, when our enemies are judges. This entails bewilderment and great intoxication where people know this yet transgress, in the manner of what the prophet said (Isaiah 29:9): ״שכרו ולא יין נעו ולא שכר״ — for they abandon the essence and give bride-price and significance to idolatry. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 16:4): ״ירבו עצבותם אחר מהרו״ — that is, they abandon the essence and give bride-price to another god to serve him. Robbery: for anyone who forsakes the laws of Israel and pursues his case in gentile courts — this is outright robbery, yet he does not regard it as robbery, and therefore he does not restore it, and consequently he has no forgiveness ever. And the reason Scripture used the expression ״תשים״ is to teach that the judge must set before the litigant that piece of evidence by which he judges him and from which he derives the ruling — and so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: ״כתבו לי מאיזה טעם דנתוני — write for me on what basis you judge me״ — they write it and give it to him." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״ואלה המשפטים אשר תשים לפניהם״ — wherever it says אלה, it invalidates the preceding ones: (Genesis 2:4) ״אלה תולדות השמים והארץ״ — it invalidates the preceding ones; He said: this one I established for Me, and this one I did not establish for Me. (ibid. 6) ״אלה תולדות נח״ — it invalidates the preceding ones: these are the generation of Enosh and the generation of the Flood. (ibid. 37) ״אלה תולדות יעקב״ — it invalidates the chiefs of Esau. But wherever it says ואלה, it adds upon the preceding ones: (Exodus 1:1) ״ואלה שמות בני ישראל״ — it adds upon the merit of the children of Reuben, the children of Simeon. So too here, ״ואלה המשפטים״ adds upon the preceding ones: just as the preceding ones were from Sinai, so too these are from Sinai. The Torah has laws before it and laws after it, and the Torah is in the middle. Laws before it — that is what is written above (ibid. 15): ״שם שם לו חק ומשפט,״ and furthermore (ibid. 18): ״ושפטו את העם בכל עת.״ Laws after it — ״ואלה המשפטים,״ and the Ten Commandments in the middle, as it is written (Proverbs 8:19–20): ״בארח צדקה אהלך בתוך נתיבות משפט.״ Another interpretation: (Psalms 147:19) ״מגיד דבריו ליעקב חקיו ומשפטיו לישראל״ — ״מגיד דבריו ליעקב,״ these are the Ten Commandments; ״חקיו ומשפטיו לישראל,״ this is ״ואלה המשפטים״; ״לא עשה כן לכל גוי ומשפטים וגו׳״ — He did not give everything except to Israel. Adam the first was commanded in six commandments. Noah came and added a seventh — this is the limb from a living animal. Abraham came and added an eighth — this is circumcision. Jacob came and added a ninth — this is the sinew of the thigh. Israel came — He gave them the Ten Commandments; He gave them everything. And this is what is written (Numbers 7:14): ״כף אחת עשרה זהב מלאה קטרת״ — this is the Torah, which was given from the palm of the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, into the palm of the hand of Moses, and it is one Torah, and they are the Ten Commandments; ״מלאה קטרת״ — in gematria תרי״ג, by the substitution of קו״ף with דל״ת in the alphabet א״ת ב״ש. When they stood at Mount Sinai and said ״כל אשר דבר ה׳ נעשה ונשמע,״ He said to them: Behold, the entire Torah is given to you. Hence: ״לא עשה כן לכל גוי ומשפטים וגו׳.״ R. Eliezer says: if there is justice below, there is no justice above; if there is no justice below, there is justice above. How so? If those below carry out justice, it is not carried out from above. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: if I were to seek even one moment to suspend justice, the world would not be able to endure, for Isaiah said, peace be upon him (Isaiah 27:4): ״אפסעה בה אציתנה יחד״ — if I step one pace in judgment, I shall burn the world entirely. Why? ״או (ibid.) יחזק במעוזי יעשה שלום לי שלום יעשה לי״ — that is, either they hold fast to the laws of My Torah. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: Israel were not destroyed except on account of Torah and justice, as it is said (Job 4:20): ״מבקר לערב יוכתו מבלי משים.״ ״אלה המשפטים אשר תשים״ — לנצח יאבדו." "<b>Which you shall set before them.</b> If he merits, it becomes for him a potion of life; if he does not merit, it becomes for him a potion of death. And the explanation of this is that this is why the expression תשים is mentioned, from the root סם (potion), to indicate that if the judge judges righteously, it is for him a potion of life, and if not, it becomes for him a potion of death. And similar to this is what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded on the verse (Deuteronomy 11:18): ״ושמתם את דברי אלה״ — it is from the root סם (potion), as they said: if a person merits through words of Torah, they become for him a potion of life; if he does not merit, they become for him a potion of death."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {4 "The phrase 'והוי למבוכה הזאת ולשכרות הגדול הזה' is textually difficult; rendered hyper-literally. The quotation from the Rabbis at the end (כתבו לי מאיזה טעם דנתוני) appears in Aramaic and is left as found."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:33.609310097Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}